16'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-15'\\\\''),('\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-26'\\\\'')]:\n rows=old.execute('\\\\''select seq,publication_day,request_sha256 from judgements where view_id=? order by seq'\\\\'',(viewer,)).fetchall()\n a={r[2] for r in rows if r[1]<cut}; b={r[2] for r in rows if r[1]>=cut}\n print(viewer,'\\\\''families before/after/reuse'\\\\'',len(a),len(b),len(a&b),'\\\\''first after'\\\\'',next((r[0],r[1]) for r in rows if r[1]>=cut))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"1 v8 v4 {'definite': 11, 'overlap': 1, 'clear': 61, 'ambiguous': 4, 'unparsed': 0, 'dates': 77, 'coverage': 0.948}\n2026-09-08 definite (419.0, 419.0, 'year') (414.3, 414.3, 'year') \npart: About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; Q3’s $108 billion total-revenue guidance midpoint at the recent 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $99.9 billion. Carrying that sequential growth into Q4 gives an H2 analyst baseline of about $212 billion; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and approximately $419 billion for FY2027. \nwhole: Q1 and Q2 total $177.82 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate forward to the $68 billion prior-year Q4 base implies about $128.5 billion in Q4, or $414.3 billion for FY2027. Allowing for the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration and Rubin ramp, but tempering for supply limits, supports roughly $414–421 billion.\n2 v9 v14 {'definite': 56, 'overlap': 0, 'clear': 12, 'ambiguous': 5, 'unparsed': 0, 'dates': 73, 'coverage': 0.932}\n3 v35 v3 {'definite': 1, 'overlap': 0, 'clear': 28, 'ambiguous': 4, 'unparsed': 0, 'dates': 33, 'coverage': 0.879}\n2026-08-26 definite (421.0, 421.0, 'year') (396.0, 396.0, 'year') \npart: Applying the latest reported FY2027 Q2 year-over-year growth rate of 117% to the cited FY2026 base of $194 billion implies about $421 billion for FY2027 ($194 billion × 2.17). Q1’s 92% growth and Q2’s $89 billion result, up 18% sequentially, support the direction; this is an extrapolation, not company guidance. \nwhole: Starting from the $396 billion FY2027 estimate, add about $4.0 billion for Q2 reported revenue above the $92.2 billion consensus and about $4.1 billion for Q3 guidance above the $103.9 billion consensus, giving roughly $404 billion. Carrying forward the prior estimate of about $40 billion of second-half Data Center compute upside implies approximately $444 billion.\nv9 missing fixed same-day []\nv14 missing fixed same-day []\nv9 2026-07-10 393B in request? False reference: No FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied, so there is no direct reference rung. The nearest company reference is $91 billion of total revenue guidance for Q2 FY2027, which is not itself an FY2027 Data Center figure and cannot be assigned a rung on this ladder.\nv14 2026-07-10 393B in request? True reference: Bloomberg-compiled projections expect FY2027 sales of $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\n- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\nv9 families before/after/reuse 4 70 0 first after (167, '2026-07-10')\nv14 families before/after/reuse 0 71 0 first after (167, '2026-07-10')\nv16 families before/after/reuse 0 56 0 first after (274, '2026-07-15')\nv21 families before/after/reuse 0 50 0 first after (542, '2026-07-26')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"I reproduced the three parser counts and found a second misparse in the validation sample. On September 8, the parser used $414.3B for the total where the answer’s final forecast was $414–421B. That pair overlaps, so it shouldn’t count as a definite contradiction. I also found a case where H3 could reject the sourced Bloomberg figure the link was meant to deliver."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"**NOT READY.** The paired arms and date cutoffs are sound. The reading rule can still pass or fail because of how forecasts are parsed, which dates enter the denominator, or where a sourced figure appears in the request. Those decisions need to be fixed before building the harness.\n\n### Arms and dry proof\n\nI verified the first affected judgements at seq **167, 274 and 542**. None of the four readers’ accepted request hashes crosses its retirement cutoff, and all four stored refusals belong to non-readers. Retiring by reader and judgement date is sound for this archive. The proof should check the archive hit **before** transport as well as the outbound allow-list. A cache hit bypasses transport, so the allow-list alone cannot prove every post-link reader answer was fresh. Bind admission to the replay’s judgement day and require fresh answers for judgements actually made on or after the cutoff. [Retirement and admission](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:24) · [Judge-day entry point](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/replay.py:119) · [Archive lookup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:69)\n\nThe **latest accepted upstream conclusion** is the right invariant. At prompt construction, capture its judgement ID and compare the rendered date, reference, and forecast against that snapshot. Check a same-day upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer, and a refused answer followed by an accepted retry. Checking the final ledger or archive call order can pick a conclusion that the reader had yet to receive. [Dry proof](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:49) · [Dependency block](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:127) · [Prompt construction and retry order](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:327)\n\n### Blocking changes to the reading rule\n\n1. **Make hand reading and coverage agree.** I reproduced the parser’s **11, 56, and 1** counts. The validation sample contains another false definite beyond old-3 August 26. On old-1 September 8, the parser takes **$414.3B** for total revenue, but the answer’s final forecast is **$414–421B**. The **$419B** segment forecast overlaps it. Under the proposed coverage rule, a clean sequence can also become inconclusive because only 15 unflagged pairs get hand-confirmed. Use the parser to order the work and hand-classify **every distinct answer pair**. Treat a misparse as a diagnostic and record the corrected class, or mark the pair unresolved. Count carried-forward dates from those classifications. [Validation trace](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/parser-validation.txt:35) · [Parser rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/contradictions.py:50) · [Hand-reading and coverage rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:62)\n2. **Use the same dates for each pair’s comparison.** Carry-forward and three paired improvements are suitable for a decision about this window. Calculate paired rates on the same jointly readable dates against the fixed **73-day** window. An 80% floor by itself can still hide enough contradictions to reverse a small improvement. If unresolved dates could reverse a pair’s result, call it inconclusive. Keep the two-day control floor as an explicit testability condition. Report conversions from definite contradiction to overlap separately from clear reconciliation. Wider ranges can reduce definite contradictions while leaving agreement unresolved. [Primary rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:69)\n3. **Trace H3 through the link to the outside source.** Old-2’s July 10 `v14` request contains Bloomberg’s **$393B** estimate in `e195`. The `v9` request has no $393B. Correctly using that externally sourced **company** figure through `v14` is part of the intended benefit. Follow the figure through the exact upstream or earlier judgement to its dated outside statement. Fail a figure supported only by a system forecast, or a company estimate presented as segment consensus. Use keywords to find candidates and specify which references are checked regardless of keywords. [H3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:84) · [July source-use check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:13)\n4. **Resolve H1–H5 and verdict precedence.** H1’s threshold is usable as an explicit tolerance. A reader whose correction is also refused is a product failure and must have a defined outcome even though replay stops. For H2, an unexplained non-reader request or substantive output difference invalidates the fixed-trajectory comparison. For H4, keep the review of every `v14` answer, and make unresolved CPU overlap consequential when the unexplained uplift supplies the apparent reconciliation. H5’s tolerance of one extra material failure per pair could likewise let a single unsupported, carried-forward forecast drive P. Define the question-date unit and how reader disagreements are resolved. A material error that supplies the apparent gain must prevent a pass. Write the precedence explicitly for confirmed harm, an invalid or incomplete run, and a completed comparison that misses P. [Outcome and harm rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:71) · [Second-refusal path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:340)\n\n### Cost and time\n\nThe **$5.8** base estimate is consistent with three controls at $0.872 and three linked sequences at about $1.05. Keeping July’s latch limits live reservations to six calls across the six sequences. Dry runs can measure request sizes and exercise propagation schedules. Future output tokens, retries, and actual propagation remain estimates. Old-2’s stored reader calls had a median of **38.2 seconds**. At that rate, 266 serial calls take about **2.8 hours** before replay and retries, so I would plan around three hours. This does not block the build once the reading and replay contracts are settled. [Cost and time](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:100) · [July latch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:56)"}}
